  "strategy": "**1. Approach and Rationale**\n\nThe core challenge is to efficiently explore the high-dimensional space of step functions h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] that integrate to 1, aiming to minimize the maximum cross-correlation with (1-h) over all shifts k. This is a constrained optimization problem with a non-convex, piecewise-linear objective. \n\nThe proposed approach combines multi-resolution refinement with derivative-free optimization (DFO) and Bayesian optimization (BO) in a staged framework. This strategy:\n- Explores lower resolutions first to escape local optima\n- Uses DFO for feasible space exploration at interpolated resolutions\n- Employs BO to parameterize and parallelize gradient information across resolutions\n- Balances exploration vs exploitation dynamically\n\nKey innovations include:\n- Progressive resolution increases with feasible interpolation\n- Hierarchical Bayesian optimization joining statistical confidence with physical metrics\n- Information-efficient exploration via model-guided sampling\n- Condorcet voting between nested intervals\n\nThe ERD\u0150S-SMART framework directly minimizes the correlation metric without smooth relaxations, maintaining fidelity to the problem definition. We leverage the exponential sensitivity in high dimensions and build a global-to-local search structure. Budget estimation shows potential for improvement while respecting the 1300s limit.\n\n**2. Mathematical Specification**\n\nVariables:\n- h(x): Step function value over [0,2], discretized to N points (N \u2208 {80, 160, 320, 400})\n- \u03b4 = 2.0/N (discretization step)\n- dx = 2.0/N (integration factor)\n- \u03a6(h) = \u03b4 * max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx (object to minimize)\n- S(h) = \u222bh(x) dx = N/2 (integral constraint)\n- T(h) = max{h(x), 1-h(x)} (clipping metric)\n- \u03b1 = #relevant lags (6% to 40% of correlation array length)\n- \u03c4 = Bayesian stepping parameter [0.5, 0.7]\n- \u03bb = smoothing weight [1e-7, 1e-5]\n- p = Bayesian optimization batch size [10, 50]\n- q = DFO perturbation size [0.01, 0.1]\n\nObjective:\nmin_{h} \u03a6(h) subject to S(h)=N/2 and 0\u2264h(x)\u22641 for all x\n\nFocuses on:\n- Minimizing shift overlapped energy\n- Maintaining integral constraint\n- Preserving function feasibility\n\nFormal transformations:\n- f(h) = -\u03a6(h) (to align with maximization)\n- lags_idx = argpartition(corr, -\u03b1) (top correlations)\n- \u2207f(h) \u2248 f(h_adj) - f(h) (adjacent score difference) [via limited derivatives]\n\n**3. Implementation Procedure**\n\nBegin with parent validation:\n\n```python\ndef validate_parent(parent, target):\n    if len(parent) != len(target) or target == -1:\n        target = len(parent) / 2\n    h = parent.astype(float)\n    diff = target - np.sum(h)\n    # Apply 50 iterations of projection\n    for _ in range(50):\n        if abs(diff) < 1e-7:\n            break\n        if diff > 0:\n            pad = max(0.05, abs(diff) / 50)\n            h_low = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n            h = np.interp(np.linspace(0,1,len(h)+int(abs(diff)/pad)), \n                          np.linspace(0,1,len(h)), \n                          h_low)\n            h = project(h, target)\n        else:\n            h_up = np.clip(h, -0.9, 0.9)\n            h = np.interp(np.linspace(-0.9,0.9,len(h)), \n                          np.linspace(0,1,len(h)), \n                          1-h_up)\n            h = project(h, target)\n    return np.clip(project(h, target), 0, 1)\n\ndef project(h, target):\n    diff = target - np.sum(h)\n    scale = diff / np.sum(h) if len(h)>0 else 0\n    return np.clip(h * (1 + scale), 0, 1) / (1 + scale) if diff != 0 else h\n```\n\nCore algorithm:\n\n```python\ndef ERDOS_SMART(n_start=80, time_limit=1300):\n    # Initialization\n    targets = [160, 320, 400]\n    temps = [0.9, 0.6, 0.5]\n    hbias = 0.3\n    lookbacks = [500, 300, 200]\n    lambs = [0.001, 0.0005, 0.0002]\n    h_init = interpolate_h(initial_h_values, n_start)\n    n_list = [n_start] + targets\n    best_h = h_init\n    best_score = \u03a6(best_h)  # Compute correlation\n\n    # Precompute all correlation values\n    all_corrs = {}\n    for n_orig in [400, 320, 160, 80]:\n        corr = np.correlate(initial_h_values[:n_orig], 1-initial_h_values[:n_orig], 'full')\n        all_corrs[n_orig] = corr * (2.0/n_orig)\n\n    # Multi-stage optimization\n    for n_idx, n_size in enumerate(n_list):\n        if time_limit - time.time() < 1200: break\n        \n        print(f\"Resolution {n_idx}: n={n_size}\")\n        # Set parameters (depends on stage)\n        psize = 20 + n_size\n        alpha = min(0.3, 0.15 * (n_idx+1))\n        eta = 0.1 + 0.05 * (n_idx)\n        \n        # Bayesian optimization loop\n        for batch in range(0, ceil(time_limit/60), 2):\n            if time_limit - time.time() < 5: break\n            \n            # Smooth cross-correlation for BO\n            smooth_score(h, n_size, alpha, lambs[n_idx])\n            \n            # Services: Model building, Sampling, Selection\n            samples = generate_candidates(best_h, n_size, eta)\n            scores = [\u03a6(s) for s in samples]\n            \n            # Condorcet method for best candidate\n            k = min(5 + len(candidates)//5, len(samples))\n            best_sample = np.median(samples[:k], axis=0)\n            if \u03a6(best_sample) < best_score:\n                best_score = \u03a6(best_sample)\n                best_h = project_feasible(best_sample, n_size/2)\n                # Update Bayesian prior + increase lookback window\n                lookbacks[n_idx] += 20\n                if np.random.rand() < 0.3: \n                    expand_to_next_resolution(n_idx+1, parent=best_h)\n                    break\n        \n        # Transition to next resolution if improvement threshold met\n        if best_score < 0.38 and random()<ha.bias:\n            expand_to_next_resolution(n_idx+1, best_h, \u03a6(best_h))\n    \n    return best_h, best_score, len(best_h)\n```\n\n**4. Search Choices and Variations**\n\nThe core strategy includes:\n\n**Core Algorithm Variations:**\n- **Resolution Expansion**: Staged increase from 80\u2192640 via Condorcet voting\n- **Derivative Replacement**: Bayesian test based on smooth correlation\n- **Orthogonal Features**: Refined lookbacks based on relative improvement\n- **Diversity Maintenance**: Perturbations preserving dime-sized blocks\n\n**Major Parameter Sets (Specify Optimizer Controls):**\n```python\nBATCH_SCHEDULING = (\n    (n_start=80, \u03bb=0.001, lookback=500),\n    (n_start=160, \u03bb=0.0005, lookback=800),\n    (n_start=320, \u03bb=0.0002, lookback=1500),\n    (n_start=400, \u03bb=0.00005, lookback=2000)\n)\n```\n\n**5. Computation Budget and Robustness**\n\nDominant costs: 15s per resolution change, ~35s per batch of 10 samples. Projection overheads of ~0.1s per fitness eval. \n\n**Budget Allocations:**\n- Coarse stage (n=80): ~250s\n- Medium stages (n=160): ~500s\n- Fine stages (n=400): ~750s\n- Condorcet filtering: ~300s\n\nTerm conditions:\n- 10-minute timer on expensive operations\n- \u03b5-improvement threshold = 1e-8 in score\n- Budget shortfall fallback: Project nearest feasible point\n\nFallback strategy:\n```python\ndef fallback_operation(parent):\n    if parent is None: return np.zeros(400)\n    n = len(parent)\n    diff = n/2 - np.sum(parent)\n    # Clip and patch\n    clipped = np.clip(parent, 0, 1)\n    target = n/2\n    while np.sum(clipped) < target:\n        pad = min(0.02, target - np.sum(clipped))\n        tidx = np.argmax(clipped)\n        clipped[tidx] = min(clipped[tidx] + pad, 1.0)\n    return clipped\n```\n\n**6. Validation and Return Contract**\n\n**Feasibility Checks:**\n1. 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n2. sum(h) == (n/2) within 1e-12\n3. No NaNs in correlation calculation\n4. np.max(corr) is finite\n\n**Re-evaluation:**\n```python\ndef validate_final(h, n):\n    if n not in [80, 160, 320, 400]: n = 400\n    h_proj = project_feasible(h, n/2)\n    dx = 2.0/n\n    corr = np.correlate(h_proj, 1-h_proj, 'full')\n    return dx * np.max(corr), len(corr)\n\n# Output format\nscore, n_final = validate_final(best_h, 400)\nassert score < 0.3809, \"Did not surpass previous record\"\n```\n\nSANITY CHECKS:\n- Ensure interpolation preserves continuity\n- Monitor gradient magnitude\n- Maintain consistency across representations\n\nThis strategy directly minimizes the correlation metric with Bayesian-assisted exploration and hierarchical resolution expansion. Extensive fallbacks guarantee a feasible candidate will be found before the budget expires.",
